export abstract anchor FactoryOnboarding:
    states:
        Specified, configured, and operational are cumulative states. Specified
        means project-owned Piton decisions and an onboarding report exist.
        Configured means specified plus a configuration, credential references,
        an approved policy record, and a descriptor when an implementation
        exists. Operational means configured plus a validated six-command
        surface and dashboard. State is derived from artifact validity, not
        artifact existence.
    outputs:
        Onboarding produces project-owned Piton durable decisions, the
        implementation configuration, credential references, a policy record,
        an existing-work import report when applicable, and an onboarding
        report. The descriptor is created only when an implementation exists.
        Descriptor and configuration writes are individually atomic, and the
        configuration keeps the previous valid file as a backup. No transaction
        spans unrelated artifacts.
    decisionSet:
        Onboarding uses a semantic decision set, not a universal schema.
        Categories are implementation and entry point for operational state;
        harnesses and account kinds with credential references; API budgets and
        subscription allowances with reset policy; build and test capacity with
        remote pools when builds are used; delivery default; routing categories,
        thresholds, and approved models when learned routing is used;
        concurrency limits; and operating-policy version.
        A category is required when its predicate is true. Not-applicable
        requires a false predicate, not an empty value. A present category must
        carry its minimum content: a binding for credentials, a limit and reset
        window for allowances, a pool and units for capacity, a destination for
        delivery, and a category with thresholds and approved models for
        routing. Fields and encoding remain project-specific.
    credentialBoundary:
        Configuration and Piton store references only, never literal secrets.
        A reference is bound to an operation, a route, and its destination or
        account. The broker resolves it under a scoped lease. Wired children do
        not inherit ambient credential environment variables and have no
        broker-less credential access. Onboarding and checks validate
        resolution and authorization, including account identity and write
        scope where the provider exposes them. A missing, revoked,
        wrong-account, or under-scoped reference blocks the affected launch or
        check. Rotation invalidates affected checks. Redaction and secret
        detection remain best effort.
    configurationChanges:
        There is no hot reload. A change is validated against the decision set,
        the descriptor, and the currently approved policy, written atomically
        with a backup, and assigned a configuration content digest. A graceful
        restart is required. The new process revalidates and loads the new
        file; if any check fails, it restores the previous file and reports the
        failure.
        Each attempt records the configuration digest it started with. Jobs,
        deliveries, claims, leases, and retry records record the same digest.
        Configuration snapshots are content-addressed and retained while any
        record references them. Running work finishes under that digest. A
        lowered cap admits no new work until committed reservations and
        unresolved exposure reconcile. Committed work is never retroactively
        invalidated.
    onboardingConformance:
        Conformance uses separately verdicted cells with an expected observation
        each: specified, configured, and operational states; each decision
        category missing under a maximal-applicability fixture; each descriptor
        field missing, wrong type, empty, traversing, or unsupported, and an
        unresolved tether; an unresolvable, revoked, wrong-account, and
        under-scoped credential plus a resolvable positive control; a planted
        secret that must fail and a clean scan that must pass; existing-work
        import counts and provenance; a successful configuration change and a
        failure after write that restores the backup; and an operational claim
        without an implementation, which must fail. Not-applicable is not a
        passing verdict for an applicable category.
